menu "Daly BMS monitor"

choice BMS_BUILD_MODE
    prompt "Build mode"
    default BMS_MODE_NORMAL
    help
      What the firmware does when it boots. The two diagnostic modes exist
      because the Daly field layouts and the CAN wiring are the two things
      that cannot be verified anywhere but on the bench.

config BMS_MODE_NORMAL
    bool "Normal - poll the packs and draw the UI"

config BMS_RAW_LOGGER
    bool "Raw CAN frame logger"
    help
      Dumps every transmitted and received CAN frame over USB serial and does
      not initialise the display.

      Use this on first contact with real hardware: the Daly field layouts in
      docs/hardware/daly-can-protocol.md come from community reverse-engineering
      rather than a vendor datasheet, so they must be checked against an actual
      BMS (cross-referenced with Daly's own app) before the GUI is trusted.

config BMS_CAN_LOOP_TEST
    bool "CAN transceiver loop test"
    help
      Bit-bangs the CAN TX pin and watches the CAN RX pin, with the TWAI
      controller out of the picture entirely. That walks a level out through
      the transceiver's driver, onto the bus, back through its receiver and
      into the MCU - so a pass proves the whole board-side chain at once:
      3V3 on the transceiver, Rs grounded, CANH/CANL wired, and TX/RX the
      right way round.

      Worth having because every one of those faults looks identical through
      TWAI: the controller cannot read back its own bits, and reports a flood
      of bus errors whichever of them is to blame. This says which.

config BMS_TOUCH_MONITOR
    bool "Capacitive touch monitor"
    help
      Prints both touch pads' benchmark, smoothed reading and the margin to the
      activation threshold, several times a second, and does nothing else.

      Touch a pad and watch the numbers. A delta that never moves means the pad
      or its wiring, not the threshold; a delta that moves but never reaches
      "need" means BOARD_TOUCH_THRESH_DIV is too high for this pad size and
      overlay.

endchoice

config BMS_INVERT_CURRENT
    bool "Daly reports discharge above the 30000 current bias"
    default n
    help
      Which way the pack current points. Daly's own protocol document does
      not say, and the Daly drivers in circulation disagree about it.

      Settle it at bring-up: put a load on a pack and watch the current on
      its detail page. It must read negative. If it reads positive, enable
      this. The overview is unaffected either way - its dial shows magnitude
      and its CHARGING badge comes from the BMS's own state - and so is the
      charging-below-freezing alarm, for the same reason.

endmenu
